 if(level>=3)out+="<section class=\"wax-stage\"><h4>③ Sum only diagonal entries to obtain the character</h4><p>Only a coefficient returning to the same basis vector enters the trace. An exchanged atom's arrow appears in another atom's row, so its sign does not contribute to this column's diagonal entry.</p>"+table("Diagonal ledger for all nine basis vectors",["Original basis vector","Diagonal coefficient of this column"],o.traceTerms.map(t=>[esc(t.basisLabel),esc(t.coefficient)]))+table("Atoms, directions, and characters are different counts",["Quantity","Returned by Python for this operation"],[["Fixed atoms",esc(o.fixedAtomCount)],["Three-dimensional polar-vector trace",esc(o.polar.trace)],["Character of the nine-dimensional displacement representation",esc(o.trace)]])+table("Complete character rows for the four operations",["Representation or count",...r.operations.map(op=>operationMath(op.id))],[["Fixed atoms",...r.characters.fixedAtoms],["Polar-vector trace",...r.characters.polar],["Axial-vector trace",...r.characters.axial],["Trace on all displacements",...r.characters.displacement]].map(row=>row.map(esc)))+"<h5>Fixed counterexample: C2 fixes oxygen, but the displacement character is not +1</h5>"+table("Three directions at fixed oxygen and six at exchanged hydrogen atoms",["Set of directions","Returned diagonal entries"],[["Oxygen O: x, y, z",esc(r.counterexample.oxygenDiagonal.join(', '))],["Six directions at the two hydrogen atoms",esc(r.counterexample.hydrogenDiagonal.join(', '))],["Full displacement character",esc(r.counterexample.actualDisplacementCharacter)]])+"<p>This C2 counterexample is a fixed reference independent of the selected operation. Polar and axial vectors have different reflection traces, so translations and rotations must be treated separately in the next step.</p><div class=\"wax-pair\"><div><h5>Translations transform as polar vectors</h5>"+math(o.polar.latex)+"</div><div><h5>Rotations transform as axial vectors</h5>"+math(o.axial.latex)+'</div></div></section>';
 if(level>=4)out+="<section class=\"wax-stage\"><h4>④ Remove rigid motions and reduce the internal vibrations</h4><p>The nine-dimensional displacement space of this nonlinear molecule contains three rigid translations, three rigid rotations, and three internal vibrations. Rotations are axial vectors, so subtracting the translation representation twice is incorrect.</p>"+math(String.raw`\Gamma_{\mathrm{vib}}=\Gamma_{3N}-\Gamma_T-\Gamma_R`)+table("Subtract character rows operation by operation",["means",...r.operations.map(op=>operationMath(op.id))],ROLES.map(role=>[esc(roleName(role)),...r.characters[role].map(esc)]))+table("C2v character table in the stated coordinates",["Irreducible type",...r.operations.map(op=>operationMath(op.id))],r.characterTable.rows.map(row=>[irrepMath(row.irrep),...row.characters.map(esc)]))+math(String.raw`m_\alpha=\frac{1}{|G|}\sum_g\chi_\alpha(g)^*\chi(g)`)+reductionTable(r,'vibration')+"<p class=\"wax-ledger-note\">The 2 in 2A₁ counts two equivalent copies, not a two-dimensional irrep. All C2v irreps are one-dimensional; symmetry does not force the two A₁ vibrations to have the same frequency.</p><details data-wax-native=\"reductions\""+(details.reductions?' open':'')+"><summary>Open the full reduction ledgers for displacements, translations, and rotations</summary>"+(details.reductions?ROLES.filter(role=>role!=='vibration').map(role=>reductionTable(r,role)).join(''):"<p>Open the displacement, translation, and rotation ledgers using the same character table.</p>")+'</details></section>';
 if(level>=5){const ch=r.selectionChannels.find(x=>x.id===channel)||r.selectionChannels[0];out+="<section class=\"wax-stage\"><h4>⑤ Explain polarization permissions through operator symmetry</h4><p>Start from a totally symmetric A₁ vibrational ground state. Electric-dipole components describe infrared coupling; polarizability components describe Raman coupling. Specify the coupling operator before testing a final state.</p>"+math(String.raw`\Gamma_f^*\otimes\Gamma_{O}\otimes\Gamma_i\supset A_1`)+select('channel',"Browse an optical channel returned in this calculation",r.selectionChannels.map(ch=>[ch.id,channelName(ch.id)]),ch.id)+table("Operator identity for the selected channel",["Optical channel","Operator component","Operator irrep","Initial-state irrep"],[[esc(channelName(ch.id)),esc(ch.operator),irrepMath(ch.operatorIrrep),irrepMath(ch.initialIrrep)]])+table("Test the totally symmetric component mode by mode in this channel",["Schematic vibration type","Final-state irrep","Products over the four operations","Numerator sum","Group order","Totally symmetric copies","Symmetry permission"],ch.modeRows.map(row=>[esc(modeName(row.modeId)),irrepMath(row.finalIrrep),esc(row.weightedProducts.join(', ')),esc(row.sum),esc(row.groupOrder),esc(row.multiplicity),'<span class="'+(row.allowed?'wax-allowed':'wax-forbidden')+'">'+(row.allowed?"Allowed in this channel":"Forbidden in this channel")+'</span>']))+table("Three modes compared across four optical channels",["Schematic vibration type","Irreducible type",...r.selectionChannels.map(ch=>esc(channelName(ch.id)))],r.vibrations.modeTypes.map((mode,i)=>[esc(modeName(mode.id)),irrepMath(mode.irrep),...r.selectionChannels.map(ch=>ch.modeRows[i].allowed?"Allowed":"Forbidden")]))+"<p>These are symmetry permissions, not peak positions, frequencies, intensities, or complete normal modes. Force constants determine the mixing of the two A₁ modes; the B₁ label follows this page's xz molecular-plane convention.</p><p>Continue in the original water animation: select a vibration and switch polarization to compare the displayed motion with its permission.</p></section>";}
